{
  "summary": "This paper introduces Prefix-conditioned SFT (P-SFT), a method to address diversity collapse in large language models (LLMs) during training and inference. The authors argue that traditional SFT causes gradient conflicts when training on 1-to-M datasets, leading to mode collapse. P-SFT decouples this by prepending semantically neutral prefixes to prompts, enabling distinct prior distributions for each response. Experiments show significant improvements in diversity and reasoning performance across multiple benchmarks.",
  "strengths": [
    "Clearly identifies a novel problem (optimization interference in SFT) and provides a principled solution.",
    "Presents a method that is both simple and effective, with empirical validation on a wide range of benchmarks.",
    "Demonstrates that the method improves both training and inference diversity, with strong ablation studies and comparisons to prior work.",
    "Shows that P-SFT can be used with existing models without requiring architectural changes or additional data."
  ],
  "weaknesses": [
    {
      "issue": "The paper does not provide a detailed theoretical analysis of why the prefixing approach specifically leads to improved diversity, despite mentioning it in the appendix.",
      "location": "section/paragraph",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "issue": "Some experimental results (e.g., pass@k and cons@k) are reported without sufficient discussion on how they compare to prior methods or what the baselines are.",
      "location": "section/paragraph",
      "severity": "minor"
    }
  ],
