{
  "summary": "This paper introduces Prefix-conditioned SFT (P-SFT), a method to enhance the diversity of responses in large language models during both training and inference. The authors argue that traditional SFT, when applied to 1-to-M datasets, causes optimization interference, leading to mode collapse. P-SFT mitigates this by using semantically neutral prefixes to decouple response learning, thereby improving diversity and reasoning performance. The method is validated through experiments on multiple reasoning and generation benchmarks, showing significant improvements in diversity metrics and test-time scaling.",
  "strengths": [
    "The paper addresses a critical issue in LLM training—diversity collapse—by identifying a root cause (optimization interference) and proposing a novel solution (P-SFT).",
    "The proposed method is simple, effective, and applicable during both training and inference without requiring complex architectural changes.",
    "Comprehensive experiments are conducted across diverse tasks and models, including ablation studies and comparisons with prior methods.",
    "The paper provides both qualitative and quantitative evidence of the method's effectiveness, including improvements in diversity metrics and reasoning performance.",
    "The authors clearly articulate the limitations and ethical implications of the method, contributing to a balanced evaluation."
  ],
  "weaknesses": [
    {
      "issue": "The paper does not provide detailed descriptions of the specific datasets or the hyperparameters used in the experiments, making it difficult to reproduce the results or assess their robustness across different settings.",
      "location": "section 4.1 and 4.2",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "issue": "The theoretical justification for the prefix mechanism is somewhat limited and lacks a deeper analysis of how the prefix interacts with the model's internal representations to achieve the observed effects.",
      "location": "section 3.2 and Appendix C",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "issue": "The paper primarily focuses on instruction-tuned and reasoning-enhanced models; the generalizability of P-SFT to other types of models or tasks is not thoroughly explored.",
      "location": "section 4.2 and 4.3",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "issue": "The evaluation of instruction following accuracy is based on automated metrics and human assessments, but the specific criteria for assessing instruction alignment are not clearly defined.",
      "location": "section 4.2",
